From: Justin Walsh <contact.me@thejustinwalsh.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 05:30:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] kv-cache: an attention op spans its batch's sequences, not the pool

With a unified pool (-np > 1) llama_kv_cache::get_n_kv padded the POOL's
highest used cell: every attention op of every sequence spanned [0,
used_max_p1), reading every other sequence's cells (masked) and every hole
below it. With the tiered cache (0028) that span decides what is staged
over PCIe: ggml_cuda_tier_stage copies every host-backed byte of the K/V
view, i.e. cells [--kv-vram-cells, n_kv), on every attention op, whoever
owns them. One idle conversation parked high in the pool made every other
conversation run from host RAM (pagoda-h6: 6-12 tok/s at 67k-113k).

A token attends only to cells holding its own sequence
(set_input_kq_mask), so the op needs to span only up to the highest cell of
the ubatch's sequences. The cells now count, per sequence, the cells each
256-cell block holds (seq_blk, kept by the same helpers as seq_pos), so
that bound is found without a scan: llama_kv_cells::seq_span_p1(), and
get_n_kv(sinfo, ubatch) pads the max over the ubatch's sequences instead
of used_max_p1(). Exact: only masked cells leave the view. The one-argument
get_n_kv (the pool bound) stays for its other caller (dsv4).

Also here, used by 0039/0040: the cells keep a lowest-free hint
(lowest_free(), amortised O(1)), a per-sequence cell count, seq_any() and
swap_cells() (exchange two cells' state; (sequence, position) pairs and
seq_pos unchanged).

API: llama_memory_seq_span_p1(mem, seq_id) (kv cache, hybrid: its attention
cache; other memory: 0). The server's tail draft size (0031, keyed on pool
cells by our 0037) now keys on the slot's own span: the tail is read for a
slot exactly when its span passes --kv-vram-cells.

LLAMA_KV_SPAN_BY_SEQ=0 restores the pool bound. The find_slot debug line
(LLAMA_KV_CACHE_DEBUG=1) prints each sequence's cells and span.
